    `Father, I come to the ordinary place today — the working hours, the unremarkable location, the daily routine that has become so familiar that I have stopped expecting You to show up in it. And I receive the burning bush today as the pattern of how You often come: not in the dramatic departure from the ordinary, but in the unusual thing at the edge of the ordinary that requires me to turn aside. Give me the Moses attention today — the willingness to notice the bush that is burning differently, to leave the main path and investigate, to turn aside from the efficiency of the route I was planning in order to see the great sight that is happening at the edge of it. When I turn aside, speak. I am turning.`
